const torqueCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'τ = F × d', note: 'Torque (moment of force) is turning effect: force times the perpendicular distance from the joint axis to the force\'s line of action, in newton-metres. Every joint the therapist mobilises, loads or guards is a torque balance. Most limb joints work as third-class levers — muscle inserts close to the axis — trading mechanical advantage for range and speed.' },
    { math: 'W = F · d · cos θ', note: 'Mechanical work is force times displacement along the force\'s direction. Only the component along the movement counts — pushing sideways does no work. The physiotherapist\'s intuition for "harder at this angle, easier at that one" is this cosine at work.' },
    { math: 'P = W / t', note: 'Power is work per unit time, in watts. Strength is a torque; power is what stairs, sprinting and falls recovery actually demand — and it is trainable separately, which is why elderly fall-prevention programmes train rate of force development, not just maximum strength.' },
    { math: 'THR = HR<sub>rest</sub> + % (HR<sub>max</sub> − HR<sub>rest</sub>)', note: 'The Karvonen formula sets training heart rate from heart-rate reserve, with HR<sub>max</sub> ≈ 220 − age (or the better Tanaka equation, 208 − 0.7 × age). Exercise prescription is physiology turned into a number the patient can watch on a pulse monitor.' },
